# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.WhatScriptureSays — the texts every later voice reads

Every later voice in this story, from the fathers to the Council of Trent to the
scholars of the last fifty years, is reading the same few pages. So the story
starts with those pages, grouped by writer, with as little comment as will let
a reader place them. How each later reader took them is for the chapters that
follow. The texts are quoted from the English Standard Version.

## Paul to the Galatians

Paul wrote to the churches of Galatia about the year 50, or a few years later.
Teachers had come after him and were urging his converts to be circumcised.
What exactly they meant by it is itself debated: the Reformers read it as a
condition added to faith, and James Dunn and N. T. Wright as a mark of who
belongs to God's people. Paul called it a different gospel:

> "I am astonished that you are so quickly deserting him who called you in the
> grace of Christ and are turning to a different gospel" (Galatians 1:6).

> "But even if we or an angel from heaven should preach to you a gospel contrary
> to the one we preached to you, let him be accursed" (1:8).

He told them how he had opposed Peter to his face at Antioch, when Peter drew
back from eating with Gentile believers, and went on:

> "a person is not justified by works of the law but through faith in Jesus
> Christ, so we also have believed in Christ Jesus, in order to be justified by
> faith in Christ and not by works of the law, because by works of the law no
> one will be justified" (2:16).

Near the end of the letter he wrote:

> "Look: I, Paul, say to you that if you accept circumcision, Christ will be of
> no advantage to you" (5:2). "You are severed from Christ, you who would be
> justified by the law; you have fallen away from grace" (5:4). "For in Christ
> Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision counts for anything, but only
> faith working through love" (5:6).

That last sentence, "faith working through love" (πίστις δι' ἀγάπης
ἐνεργουμένη), is one that the Reformers and the Council of Trent would both
claim. The chapter "Faith working through love" follows what each made of it.

## Paul to the Romans

A few years later, about 57, Paul wrote to a church he had never visited, and set
out his gospel at length.

> "For we hold that one is justified by faith apart from works of the law"
> (Romans 3:28).

He took Abraham as his example, the father of Israel:

> "Now to the one who works, his wages are not counted as a gift but as his due.
> And to the one who does not work but believes in him who justifies the
> ungodly, his faith is counted as righteousness" (4:4–5).

Abraham was counted righteous, Paul adds, "not after, but before he was
circumcised" (4:10). And of the remnant of Israel chosen by grace, he wrote:

> "But if it is by grace, it is no longer on the basis of works; otherwise grace
> would no longer be grace" (11:6).

## The letter to the Ephesians

The letter bears Paul's name. Those who hold it his date it to his imprisonment
in Rome, about 60 to 62; many others date it later in the century.

> "For by grace you have been saved through faith. And this is not your own
> doing; it is the gift of God, not a result of works, so that no one may boast"
> (Ephesians 2:8–9).

## Jesus, in the Gospels

The Gospels were written down in the second half of the first century, and they
record what Jesus said. To the woman who wept over His feet in a Pharisee's
house, whose sins He had forgiven:

> "Your faith has saved you; go in peace" (Luke 7:50).

He told a parable "to some who trusted in themselves that they were righteous".
A Pharisee in the temple thanked God that he fasted twice a week and gave tithes
of all he got.

> "But the tax collector, standing far off, would not even lift up his eyes to
> heaven, but beat his breast, saying, 'God, be merciful to me, a sinner!' I tell
> you, this man went down to his house justified, rather than the other" (Luke
> 18:13–14).

When the crowd at Capernaum asked Him, "What must we do, to be doing the works of
God?", He answered:

> "This is the work of God, that you believe in him whom he has sent" (John
> 6:29).

He said other things that the Council of Trent would quote. At the end of the
Sermon on the Mount:

> "Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven,
> but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven" (Matthew 7:21).

And to the rich young man who asked what good deed he must do to have eternal
life:

> "If you would enter life, keep the commandments" (Matthew 19:17).

## James

The letter of James has no date of its own. Douglas Moo dates it to the mid-40s,
before any letter of Paul's; others put it late in the century. James asks what
good it is "if someone says he has faith but does not have works" (2:14), and
does nothing for a brother or sister "poorly clothed and lacking in daily food".

> "So also faith by itself, if it does not have works, is dead" (James 2:17).

He too took Abraham as his example, at the binding of Isaac:

> "Was not Abraham our father justified by works when he offered up his son Isaac
> on the altar?" (2:21). "You see that a person is justified by works and not by
> faith alone" (2:24).

This is the only verse in the New Testament where the words "faith alone"
(πίστεως μόνον) appear, and they are denied. The chapter "James 2:24" weighs
what James means by them.

## What comes next

Each chapter of the story that follows hears the voices of its own time, so
that a reply never comes before what it answers: first the church's readers of
Paul from the 350s to the 1270s, then the Reformers and their Catholic
opponents from 1521 to 1541, then the council itself in 1547.
